>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Postexercise cold water&#xD;immersion demonstrated improved trends in measures of&#xD;muscle damage and soreness, as well as reducing proinflammatory&#xD;cytokine expression after resistance exercise, as&#xD;compared with a passive control condition. We can therefore&#xD;recommend athletes to use postexercise water immersion for&#xD;up to 2 days before competition or during periods with a high&#xD;density of training sessions where reducing the negative&#xD;symptoms associated with resistance exercise is preferred. The&#xD;use of postexercise HWI can also be used to obtain small-tomoderate&#xD;relative (%) increases in exercise-induced proinflammatory&#xD;cytokine responses in IL-1b, IL-8, and IL-17a, as&#xD;well as large relative (%) increases in HSP72 after resistance&#xD;exercise. As such, the use of a HWI protocol (15 minutes 3&#xD;39.1 6 0.5° C) as used in this study may promote systemic&#xD;inflammatory responses that influence downstream muscle&#xD;protein synthesis. Hot water immersion can be used as an&#xD;additional postexercise strategy to promote muscular adaptation&#xD;during preparatory periods or in-season where sessions&#xD;are scheduled more than 2 days (.38 hours) away from the&#xD;competition.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
